Chapter 21
Curtis reached U.A. before the gates were fully busy. He badged through, nodded at the tired security guard, and headed straight for the classroom. The halls were still mostly empty. He dropped into his seat, set his bag down, and rested his head on his folded arms.
The bandage was gone. A thin, pale scar crossed his palm where the knife had gone through. It didn’t pull when he flexed his fingers. He closed his eyes.
Sleep came easier at the desk than it had on the new mattress. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet. Outside, the campus slowly woke up. He didn’t move. By the time the first footsteps and voices reached the hallway, Curtis was already under, braids spilling over his arms, the scarred hand loose on the desk while the rest of Class 1-A started to arrive.
The classroom door slid open. Mina was first, mid-sentence with Uraraka, and stopped when she saw the tall shape already folded over the desk.
“No way. He’s actually asleep.”
Uraraka’s cheeks went pink on sight. She stepped closer, voice soft. “He came in early just to nap? After yesterday?”
Jiro followed, earjacks twitching at the even breathing. “Hand’s healed. Scar’s new, though.” She didn’t touch it this time. Just looked.
Kaminari peered over her shoulder. “Dude fought a Nomu, got stabbed, and still beat our high scores. Of course he’s napping like it’s a normal Tuesday.”
Iida’s engines idled as he took in the scene, torn between protocol and the fact that Curtis was clearly exhausted. “We should let him rest until Aizawa arrives. Quietly.”
Mina grinned and carefully set a small packaged melon pan on the corner of the desk. “Breakfast tax for the cryptid. Don’t wake him.”
They settled into their seats with less noise than usual. Curtis didn’t stir. The scarred hand stayed loose. The low, steady pull in his chest remained quiet while the rest of the class filtered in and the room slowly filled around the one student who was already asleep at his desk.
The class was still trading quiet comments about the news clips—pixelated shots of the USJ, their own faces half-caught on camera—when the door opened.
Aizawa stepped in covered in bandages. Arm in a sling. Half his face wrapped. He looked like he should still be in the infirmary. The room went silent.
“My injuries are irrelevant,” he said, voice flat. “Something more important is coming. The U.A. Sports Festival.”
A few students shifted. Mina raised a hand. “Isn’t it too soon? After the attack—”
“The Festival is one of the most-watched events in the world,” Aizawa cut in. “Pro agencies scout it. Canceling would cost every one of you a chance to be seen. Security will be tighter. That’s the compromise. Prepare.”
He stopped beside Curtis’s desk. The exchange student hadn’t moved. Aizawa reached out and flicked the back of his head.
“Wake up, Beardy.”
Curtis lifted his head, eyes half-lidded, scarred hand flexing once. “Mm. Festival. Got it.”
He dropped his chin back onto his arms. Aizawa didn’t push it. He turned to the rest of the class, bandages creaking.
“Two weeks. Use them. The world will be watching whether you’re ready or not.”
The room buzzed again—excitement, nerves, Midoriya already scribbling notes. Curtis stayed where he was, eyes closed, while the announcement settled over Class 1-A and the low, steady pull in his chest remained quiet.
The cafeteria was louder than usual. Class 1-A had claimed two tables and were deliberately talking about anything except the USJ. Festival brackets, costume tweaks, who might get scouted first. Ochaco stood up so fast her chair scraped.
“I’m gonna do my absolute best!” Her voice carried over the noise. Cheeks already pink, eyes bright. “I’m aiming for the top, so you all better bring it too!”
Mina whooped. Kirishima grinned and pounded the table. Even Bakugo snorted instead of exploding. Ochaco dropped back down, still buzzing, and finished her lunch in record time. When Izuku and Tenya stood to leave, she went with them.
They walked the quieter hall toward the courtyard. Izuku glanced over. “You’ve been really fired up since the announcement. More than usual. Is there a specific reason?”
Ochaco’s pace slowed. She rubbed the back of her neck, the game-face slipping into something smaller. “I… want to become a hero for the money.” She said it fast, then kept going before they could react. “My family’s construction company isn’t doing great. Jobs are slow. If I can get licensed and start sending money home, it actually helps. That’s the plan.”
Izuku’s eyes widened. Tenya’s engines idled as he processed it. “Your Quirk would be exceptionally useful in construction once you’re licensed,” Tenya said. “Zero-gravity could reduce equipment needs and labor costs significantly. They might even sell heavier machinery.”
“Dad told me not to worry about the company,” Ochaco answered, softer. “He said to chase what I wanted. So I’m doing both. Hero work and helping them.”
Izuku’s expression gentled. “That’s more practical than most reasons I’ve heard. It’s a good one.”
Tenya nodded, formal and sincere. “It is admirable. You have a clear goal and a personal stake. I respect it.”
Ochaco’s cheeks went pink again, but she smiled. “Thanks. Just… don’t tell everyone yet, okay?”
Back in the cafeteria Mina twisted in her seat, scanning the room. “Okay, where’s Curtis? He was passed out on his desk and now he’s not even here for food.”
Jiro’s earjacks twitched toward the doors. “Yard or the roof. He pulled the same thing last time we had a free period.”
Kaminari leaned over. “Think he’s avoiding the Festival hype?”
“Or sleeping through it,” Mina said. She glanced at the empty spot where he usually sat. “Someone should check he didn’t wander off campus again.”
Uraraka, still walking with Izuku and Tenya, felt her phone buzz with the group chat and didn’t open it yet. The pink on her cheeks lingered as she looked back toward the building, wondering which quiet corner the exchange student had claimed this time.
All Might spotted the familiar shape under the same tree Curtis had used before. The exchange student was slumped against the trunk, arms loose, eyes closed, uniform rumpled. All Might approached at a normal walk instead of a bound, cape settling.
“Ah, young Beardy.”
Curtis opened one eye. Red. He took in the Number One Hero standing over him, then pushed up and stretched, spine popping. “All Might. What’s up?”
“Checking on my students,” All Might said, the big smile present but quieter. “You were absent from the cafeteria. And from most of the morning, if Aizawa’s report is accurate.”
“Slept at the desk. Then out here.” Curtis rolled his neck. The scar on his palm flashed when he flexed his hand. “Festival’s in two weeks. Figured I’d rest while I could.”
All Might’s eyes flicked to the scar, then back to his face. “Recovery Girl mentioned you left the infirmary early. And that your blood type makes treatment… complicated.”
“It’s handled.” Curtis’s tone stayed even. “You looking for Midoriya? He went toward the courtyard with Uraraka and Iida.”
“I am.” All Might studied him a moment longer. “The Festival will be watched by the entire country. If you intend to compete at the level you showed against that Nomu, the agencies will notice. So will everyone else.”
Curtis shrugged one shoulder. “I’ll do what I do. Same as always.”
All Might nodded, the smile warming again. “Then rest while you can, young man. And try not to vanish every lunch. Your classmates worry.”
“Noted.” Curtis dropped back against the tree as All Might turned toward the courtyard. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet while the Symbol of Peace went to find Midoriya and the afternoon kept moving around the yard.
Curtis stayed against the tree instead of letting All Might walk off. He opened both eyes.
“Hey, All Might. Got a question.” His accent sat low. “At my current level, would it be fair if I participate? I’m thinking of pulling out. The others worked hard to get here. I was put in because of a unique case. Feels like they should all have a real shot without some freak like me setting a bar so high it kills their chances before they start.”
All Might turned fully. The smile eased into something more serious. He studied Curtis for a moment—the scarred hand, the tired set of his shoulders, the flat honesty in the question.
“You believe stepping aside would protect their dreams.”
“Something like that.”
All Might crouched so they were closer to eye level, cape pooling. “The Festival is not only about winning. It is about being seen. Your classmates will face stronger opponents than you for the rest of their careers. Hiding the level that exists does not help them prepare. It only delays the moment they meet it.” His voice stayed warm, but firm. “You were accepted because you belong here. Competing is not an insult to their effort. Refusing to compete because you are strong is its own kind of unfairness.”
Curtis was quiet. The tree bark pressed against his back. All Might stood again, the smile returning, smaller.
“Think on it. But do not decide their limits for them. They have earned the right to test themselves against whatever stands in front of them. Including you.”
He gave a short nod and headed toward the courtyard. Curtis watched him go, the question still sitting in his chest, while the low, steady pull remained as quiet as ever.
Curtis moved before All Might had fully turned away.
He dashed—clean, no warning—and drove his fist into All Might’s stomach. The impact landed solid. All Might’s boots slid back half a step on the grass. Air left him in a short grunt. Curtis stayed in close, scarred hand still pressed to the hero’s midsection, eyes level.
“No student would get the drop on you like I just did.” His voice stayed even. “I’m not saying this because I’m cocky. I’m saying it because I know my skill level.”
All Might’s hand came up and caught Curtis’s wrist, gentle but firm. He straightened, the smile gone, eyes sharp. The hit had registered. Not enough to injure. Enough to prove the point.
“You have made it.” All Might’s tone was low. He released the wrist. “And you are still a student of this school. The Festival is where the country watches what U.A. produces. If you believe your level is that far above your classmates, then show them the gap so they can decide what to do with it. Do not remove yourself and call it fairness.”
Curtis stepped back. His hand dropped. The yard was quiet around them. All Might held his gaze a moment longer, then nodded once—acknowledgment, not agreement—and continued toward the courtyard. Curtis remained under the tree, the echo of the hit still in his knuckles, while the low, steady pull in his chest stayed exactly where it always was.
Curtis didn’t move from the tree. His voice stayed flat.
“I’m dropping out. That’s final. I’ll tell Nezu myself.” He met All Might’s eyes. “If my classmates want to argue, they can fight me. Or you find one student who can push me the way that Nomu did by the end of the day. Do that and I’ll participate. I’ll even try. But I’m not chasing glory or a number-one spot. That’s what a Symbol of Peace does. I’m going to execute justice.”
All Might was quiet for a long moment. The smile was gone. He studied the student in front of him—the scarred hand, the tired honesty, the same conviction that had kept him in the plaza until his body gave out.
“You believe the Festival is glory,” he said at last. “And that justice requires you to stand apart from it.” His cape shifted. “I will not force you. But I will not hunt for a classmate who can break you either. That is not my role, and it is not theirs.” He turned fully toward the courtyard path. “Tell Nezu if that is your choice. And be prepared for your class to disagree with you more loudly than I will.”
He walked away. Curtis stayed under the tree, the decision settled in his chest, while the low, steady pull remained quiet and the afternoon continued without him.
Nezu stepped out from behind the far side of the tree line, paws folded behind his back, small smile already in place. He had clearly been there long enough.
“My my. How long has it been since someone got the drop on you like that, All Might?”
All Might stopped. Curtis’s eyes opened fully. Nezu’s gaze moved between them, bright and unreadable.
“I was enjoying the afternoon air,” the principal continued, tone light. “And then I witnessed a student land a clean strike on the Symbol of Peace. Quite the statement.” He looked at Curtis. “I also heard the rest. You intend to withdraw from the Festival and inform me yourself.”
Curtis straightened against the tree. “Yes, sir.”
Nezu’s smile didn’t change. “Then we should speak properly. My office. Both of you, if All Might is willing. I find these conversations go better when everyone who was struck is present.”
All Might exhaled, the tension in his shoulders easing a fraction. “Lead the way.”
Nezu turned and started across the yard at an easy pace. Curtis pushed off the tree and followed. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet while the three of them left the shade and headed for the main building, the Festival decision no longer just a private talk under a tree.
All Might caught Midoriya just outside the courtyard and steered him toward the main building with a large hand on his shoulder. “A moment of your time, young Midoriya. Principal Nezu’s office.”
Midoriya blinked, confused, but followed. Inside, Nezu was already behind his desk. Curtis sat in one of the chairs, legs stretched out, looking like he’d rather still be under the tree. All Might took the seat beside him. Midoriya hovered until Nezu gestured, then sat.
Curtis glanced over, expression easy. “Hey, Midoriya. What’d you have for lunch today? I had a nap.”
Midoriya’s brain stalled. “U-uh… rice and the cafeteria curry? And some fruit Uraraka shared. Why are we—?”
“Curtis has decided he may withdraw from the Sports Festival,” Nezu said, pleasant as ever. “He believes his current level would unfairly affect his classmates’ chances. He also struck All Might to demonstrate that level. We are discussing whether that reasoning holds.”
Midoriya’s eyes went wide. He looked from Curtis to All Might to Nezu, mouth open. Curtis just shrugged, scarred hand resting on his knee.
“Still curious about the curry, though. Any good?”
The office stayed quiet except for the soft tick of Nezu’s clock while Midoriya tried to process why he’d been pulled into a meeting that had started with a punch and a nap.
Midoriya found his voice. “You’re dropping out? Because you think you’re too strong?”
Curtis nodded. “Nomu pushed me. None of you have. Yet. If I enter, I set a ceiling most of the class can’t reach in two weeks. That’s not fair to the work you all put in.”
All Might’s arms were crossed. “And I told him the Festival is where they learn what that ceiling looks like.”
Nezu’s smile stayed mild. “Both positions have merit. Midoriya, you faced him in the Shipwreck Zone. You saw what he did in the plaza. Do you believe his presence would erase your classmates’ opportunities, or sharpen them?”
Midoriya’s hands clenched on his knees. He thought of the zero-pointer, the USJ, the way Curtis had told him he was strong. “I… I think we’d rise to it. Or try. Pulling out so we don’t have to face him feels like deciding we can’t before we start.” He looked at Curtis. “You said you’d enter if someone could push you like the Nomu did. What if we can’t? Do we just never get to test ourselves against you?”
Curtis was quiet for a second. The scarred hand flexed. “That’s the question, isn’t it.”
Nezu folded his paws. “Then we have until the end of the day for someone to try. If no one can, Curtis withdraws and I will accept it. If someone can, he competes. Does that satisfy the terms you offered, Beardy?”
Curtis met the principal’s eyes. “It does.”
All Might exhaled. Midoriya’s heart was already racing. Outside the office the lunch period was ending, and Class 1-A still had no idea their missing classmate had just turned the Festival into a challenge with a deadline.
Nezu’s ears perked. The mild smile sharpened into something more precise.
“Then we adjust the terms.” He set his paws on the desk. “The challenge will not stay inside Class 1-A. Every student at U.A.—Hero Course, General Studies, Support, Business—will be told that the exchange student who fought the Nomu is considering withdrawal from the Festival. Anyone who believes they can push him to that same level has until the end of the day to try. One success, he competes. None, he steps aside.”
Midoriya’s chair creaked as he leaned forward. “The whole school? Sir, that’s—”
“Honest,” Nezu finished. “The Festival is a national stage. If Beardy’s level is the question, then the entire student body deserves the chance to answer it. Limiting the pool protects no one.”
All Might’s arms stayed crossed. “You’re certain this won’t turn into a line of injured students?”
“Gym Gamma will be cleared and monitored,” Nezu said. “Recovery Girl will be on standby. Curtis will be there at 3 p.m. I will send the alert myself.” He looked at Curtis. “You offered the condition. Do you accept it at this scale?”
Curtis met his eyes, expression even. “I do.”
Midoriya’s hands had gone tight on his knees. “What do we tell the class?”
“The truth,” All Might answered. He stood, cape shifting. “I’ll make the announcement with Nezu’s notice. They should hear it from us before the alerts hit their phones.”
Nezu was already typing. The office felt smaller. Midoriya’s heart hammered while Curtis remained still in his chair, the scarred hand resting on his knee, and the quiet knowledge settled over all four of them: by the end of the day every student on campus would know the foreigner who had matched a Nomu was daring them to prove he belonged on the Festival stage.
The alert hit every U.A. device at once.
Principal Nezu:

The exchange student who engaged the Nomu at the USJ is considering withdrawal from the Sports Festival. Any student who believes they can push him to that level may challenge him in Gym Gamma until 6 p.m. today. One success and he competes. Recovery Girl is on site.
Class 1-A’s phones lit while they were still in the hallway. Bakugo’s palms crackled immediately. “Finally. I’ve been waiting to blow that extra apart.” Kirishima grinned, hardening already flickering. Todoroki read it twice, expression unreadable, then pocketed the phone. Midoriya’s hands shook. Mina and Uraraka exchanged looks—excited and worried in the same breath.
Class 1-B’s group chat exploded. Monoma laughed so hard he had to sit down. “The famous 1-A Nomu fighter? Perfect. We’ll show them their ceiling isn’t that high.” Tetsutetsu was already heading for the gym. Kendo sighed and followed to keep the damage reasonable.
In the third-year hall, Mirio Togata read the alert over Tamaki’s shoulder and lit up. “A first-year who fought a Nomu? I gotta see that! Maybe even try!” Tamaki paled and tried to sink into his hoodie. Nejire floated closer, eyes sparkling. “He sounds interesting! I’m going!”
Other names surfaced across the building—Shinso from General Studies, already calculating how his quirk might land; a few Support Course students who wanted data more than a win; even a couple of Business Course kids who just wanted to say they’d stood in the ring. By the time the clock hit 2:50, Gym Gamma’s doors were propped open and a crowd had started to form. The challenge was live. Curtis was already inside, waiting.
